<p>Oh, that <em>is</em> fascinating. There's really no reason it'd have to be limited to R<sup>3</sup>, either.</p>
<p>Say, it's complementary to an oblate spheroid in R<sup>4</sup> along a given span of the <em>t</em> axis, centered on &quot;now&quot;. So the object does not exist <em>now</em>, but it does exist in its current location for a given period of time into the past and future.</p>
<p>That's bound to screw with reality in interesting ways.</p>

